# tests/T26.bd - T26: DOES ANYTHING BUT YOUR OWN LINES
# FEED THE METER? The connected / live-trace / cooling-trace idle floor.
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE QUESTION
# ============================================================================
#   With the script doing the exact same trivial work every second, does
#   get_cpu()'s idle floor read HIGHER while a connection is open and its
#   trace is counting down than it does with nothing connected?
#
# ============================================================================
# WHY IT MATTERS
# ============================================================================
#   Every model of the CPU meter in this corpus assumes the meter is fed by
#   ONE thing: lines this script executed. Nothing official says that. A live
#   trace, an IDS sweep and an open socket are all engine-side simulation
#   state, and the official docs list them neither as CPU contributors nor as
#   non-contributors. Nobody has ever measured a connected idle floor - no
#   prior script in tests/ has opened a connection at all.
#
#   The corpus keeps circling the same asymmetry. field_notes.txt once
#   reframed the whole variance mystery as "specifically tied to scenarios
#   that actually reach the CONNECTED branch and interact with a real target",
#   then the 5x repeat block (+40.7 / +111.0 / +40.3 / +65.5 / +64.3s)
#   contradicted it - all five died in pure local token parsing, no socket
#   open. The reframe was dropped without ever being measured.
#
#   But note WHAT that block could not rule out. A trace does not end when you
#   disconnect: traces() keeps the host listed with status "cooling" and a
#   remaining countdown, and utils/net.bd's wait_for_trace_recovery() exists
#   precisely because that state outlives the script that created it. That is
#   the only known piece of game state that (a) persists across script runs,
#   (b) decays on real wall-clock time, and (c) nobody has ever checked for a
#   CPU cost. Five back-to-back runs against a host you have been connecting
#   to all session are five runs starting at five different points on that
#   cooling curve. If a cooling trace costs even one tick per second, that is
#   a run-to-run variance source hiding in plain sight - which is why PHASE C
#   below (cooling, disconnected) matters as much as PHASE B (connected).
#
# ============================================================================
# DESIGN - ONE INSTRUMENT, THREE ENVIRONMENTS, ONE RUN
# ============================================================================
#   Three phases, all measured by the SAME function poll() called with a
#   different one-character label. The 3-line loop body is therefore provably
#   identical across phases - same str() calls, same concatenations, same
#   tick cost - so the instrument cancels out and the whole comparison lives
#   inside a single run. Nothing is compared across runs.
#
#     PHASE A  10 polls  nothing connected, no trace          (the baseline)
#     PHASE B  10 polls  connection open, trace counting down
#     PHASE C  12 polls  disconnected, trace now "cooling"    (the sleeper)
#
#   poll()'s body is deliberately the same shape as test_cpu_decay.bd's idle
#   loop (sleep(1) + one log + one increment), the loop that produced this
#   investigation's known floor of a rock-steady 10.0. So PHASE A is directly
#   comparable to an existing, replicated measurement, not just to itself.
#
#   get_trace_time(), traces(), ids_status() and server_specs() are called
#   ONLY on the bracketing MARK lines, never inside a poll loop. The B-IN and
#   B-OUT marks print the live trace countdown either side of PHASE B, which
#   is all the proof needed that the trace was genuinely running during it -
#   at the cost of nothing inside the instrument itself.
#
#   PART N is the null control: the identical script with every connect /
#   ids_status / server_specs call skipped, same phase lengths, same sleeps,
#   same labels, no connection ever opened. It measures how much of any
#   A -> B -> C pattern is simply the meter drifting with elapsed script
#   runtime. Run it; without it a rising pattern is uninterpretable.
#
# ============================================================================
# HOW TO READ THE PASTE - THE PRIMARY STATISTIC IS NOT THE MEDIAN
# ============================================================================
#   The meter appears to be QUANTISED. Every steady reading on record is a
#   multiple of 10 (the live in-game meter has only ever shown multiples of
#   10; the idle floor is 10.0 + float noise, never 3.0 or 7.0). On a base
#   tier 10 CPU one tick == 10 meter points, so the smallest environmental
#   contribution this instrument can resolve as a VALUE CHANGE is a full
#   tick per second.
#
#   So do not average. For each phase report TWO numbers:
#     (1) the MODAL value        - what most samples read
#     (2) the ELEVATED COUNT     - how many of that phase's samples read
#                                  ABOVE phase A's modal value
#   A sub-tick environmental cost cannot move (1) but WILL move (2): it shows
#   up as the floor flickering one step up on some fraction of the samples
#   (e.g. A 0/10 elevated, B 4/10 elevated) while the modal value stays 10.0.
#   That fraction is the finest measurement this instrument can make, and it
#   is invisible to a median.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT EACH POSSIBLE RESULT MEANS
# ============================================================================
#   1. INERT (the prediction). A, B and C all share one modal value, elevated
#      counts all zero or all equal. -> Only your own executed lines move the
#      meter. Closes "a connected line is intrinsically more expensive than
#      the same line run locally" as an explanation for the existing-target
#      vs missing-target asymmetry, and closes the connected branch as a
#      suspect in the variance mystery. A clean, load-bearing negative.
#
#   2. STANDING CONNECTION COST. B's modal >= A's modal + 10, sustained over
#      most of PHASE B, and C falls back to A's level. -> An open socket is a
#      standing per-second cost. Every connected line in menu.bd is then more
#      expensive than the identical local line, and every throttle() inside a
#      connected block has less headroom than its threshold implies.
#
#   3. THE TRACE, NOT THE SOCKET (the jackpot). B elevated AND C elevated,
#      with C decaying back toward A as the cooling countdown runs down, A
#      lowest. -> Engine-side trace simulation feeds the player's CPU meter.
#      This is a cross-run variance source: a script started while a trace is
#      cooling begins with less headroom than the identical script started
#      after it cleared, with zero code or data difference between the runs.
#      That is exactly the shape of the +40.7 / +111.0 / +40.3 / +65.5 /
#      +64.3s spread. If this is what comes back, the next test is obvious -
#      run the same script at t+0s, t+60s and t+180s after a disconnect.
#
#   4. SUB-TICK CONTRIBUTION. Modal values all equal, but the elevated counts
#      differ materially between phases. -> Real, smaller than one tick per
#      second. Same interpretation as 2 or 3 depending on which phases are
#      elevated, just weaker; repeat with longer phases to firm it up.
#
#   5. CONNECT() TRANSIENT ONLY. One, maybe two elevated samples at the very
#      start of PHASE B, then back to A's floor for the rest. -> That is just
#      connect()'s own documented 5 ticks decaying away. NOT a standing cost.
#      Read it as outcome 1, and as a free confirmation of T08's decay curve.
#
#   6. DRIFT / ARTEFACT. PART N reproduces the same A -> B -> C pattern with
#      no connection at any point. -> The pattern is elapsed script runtime,
#      not the environment, and every phase comparison in this script is void.
#      Report it loudly; it would also compromise any other test that assumes
#      the floor is stationary over a minute.
#
# ============================================================================
# OUTPUT FORMAT
# ============================================================================
#   Poll lines:   <PH> <n> cpu=<c> t=<s>
#     PH and n come FIRST on purpose: a truncated or half-written final line
#     still names the phase and index it died in.
#   MARK lines:   MARK <tag> t= tt= ntr= st= rem= noise= cpu=
#     tag   PRE / B-IN / B-OUT / C-IN / C-OUT
#     tt    get_trace_time() - the live countdown
#     ntr   len(traces()) - how many traces the engine is tracking
#     st    traces()[0].status - "active" / "paused" / "cooling"
#     rem   traces()[0].remaining
#   MARK PRE is also the CLEAN-START CHECK: if ntr is not 0 at PRE, a trace
#   was already cooling before this script ran. Say so when you paste - that
#   makes it a dirty-start run, which is interesting data but not a baseline.
#   CONNECT line: whether the socket opened AND whether login actually worked
#     (connect() returns success:true with an empty user on bad credentials -
#     see utils/dashboard.bd; an unauthenticated session is not the condition
#     this test means by "connected", hence the ABORT-NOAUTH guard).
#   SPECS line:   the remote box's real cpu_tier / cpu_ticks / ram slots.
#     No trace in this entire investigation has ever recorded a real
#     cpu_ticks value from anywhere. Free calibration data, 1 tick.
#   Last line is always either T26 DONE or an ABORT-* line naming the reason.
#   Its absence means the engine killed the script - report that too.
#
# ============================================================================
# HOW TO RUN  (@param binds POSITIONALLY - no -flag= syntax)
# ============================================================================
#   Pick a host you have ALREADY CRACKED and pass user@ip so the connection
#   authenticates - a bare ip never auto-authenticates. 94.140.22.83 is the
#   preferred target: it is the existing-target host the whole 5-run variance
#   block was measured against.
#
#     1) Fresh session, nothing else running (check with `ps` and `jobs`),
#        and check `traces` is EMPTY before you start - run 1 must be clean.
#     2) T26 N                        (~70s) - paste it ALL
#        The null control FIRST, while the trace state is still clean.
#     3) T26 C root@94.140.22.83      (~70s) - paste it ALL
#     4) T26 C root@94.140.22.83      (~70s) - paste it ALL
#   Three runs, ~3.5 minutes of script time, no waiting required between them.
#   Run 4 is deliberately started while run 3's trace is still cooling: its
#   MARK PRE line will show ntr=1 st=cooling, and its PHASE A is then a
#   COOLING-TRACE baseline rather than a clean one. Comparing run 4's PHASE A
#   against run 3's PHASE A is a direct, free cross-run test of exactly the
#   carryover that outcome 3 predicts - do not skip it, and do not wait for
#   recovery first. Note in the paste which runs showed ntr=0 at PRE.
#   OPTIONAL 4th run against a DIFFERENT cracked host, only if runs 3-4 came
#   back elevated: it tells you whether the amount is target-specific.
#
#   If the run ends on ABORT-TRACE, the target's own trace ceiling is too
#   tight for a 10-second connected window - pick another host. PHASE A's ten
#   lines are still valid data in every abort case; paste them anyway.
# @param part "C = connect probe, N = null control that never connects" default=C
# @param host "user@ip of an account you have already cracked (ignored when part=N)" optional

# The instrument. ONE definition, called three times with a one-character
# label, so the measured loop is textually identical in every phase.
func poll(ph, reps, t0):
    var n = 0
    while n < reps:
        sleep(1)
        log(ph + " " + str(n) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
        n = n + 1

# A sleep BETWEEN the two definitions, deliberately. Whether a never-called
# func definition costs tick budget is an open question (T17); if it does,
# these two bodies back to back plus the launch lines would be an ~18-line
# unpaced burst at startup - the exact shape that has killed scripts in this
# project before. Two seconds here removes the only real kill risk in the file.
sleep(2)

# Bracketing readout. sleep(1) is its FIRST line, per this codebase's
# hard-won rule that a function called from a busy spot must pace on the way
# IN, not deeper inside its body. The second sleep caps the unpaced run at 6
# lines. Everything it calls is documented 0 ticks.
func mark(tag, t0):
    sleep(1)
    var tl = traces()
    var st = "-"
    var rem = 0
    if len(tl) > 0:
        st = tl[0].status
        rem = tl[0].remaining
    sleep(1)
    log("MARK " + tag + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " tt=" + str(get_trace_time()) + " ntr=" + str(len(tl)) + " st=" + st + " rem=" + str(rem) + " noise=" + str(get_noise()) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))

# entry_cpu is a secondary observable, not the headline: it reads the meter
# right after the file's own declarations, so a high value means declaring
# this file cost real budget. floor_cpu after the sleep is the actual baseline
# PHASE A starts from.
var c_entry = get_cpu()
sleep(3)
var PART = get_param("part", "C").replace("\"", "").trim()
var HOST = get_param("host", "").replace("\"", "").trim()
var t0 = get_unix_time()
log("T26 part=" + PART + " host=" + HOST + " entry_cpu=" + str(c_entry) + " floor_cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
mark("PRE", t0)
poll("A", 10, t0)

sleep(2)
var conn = {"success": false, "user": "", "trace_time": 0, "hostname": "-"}
if PART == "C":
    try:
        conn = connect(HOST, 22)
    catch e:
        log("CONNECT-RAISED " + str(e.message))
sleep(2)
log("CONNECT ok=" + str(conn.success) + " user=" + str(conn.user) + " tcap=" + str(conn.trace_time) + " box=" + str(conn.hostname))
sleep(2)

if PART == "C" and not conn.success:
    log("ABORT-NOCONN " + HOST + " - port never opened. PHASE A is valid, paste it.")
    return
if PART == "C" and conn.user == "":
    log("ABORT-NOAUTH socket opened but login failed - pass user@ip for a CRACKED account. PHASE A is valid, paste it.")
    disconnect(conn)
    return
if PART == "C" and conn.trace_time < 60:
    log("ABORT-TRACE ceiling only " + str(conn.trace_time) + "s, too tight for a 10s window - pick another host. PHASE A is valid, paste it.")
    disconnect(conn)
    return
sleep(1)

var ids0 = "-"
if PART == "C":
    ids0 = ids_status(conn)
sleep(2)
var sp = {"cpu_tier": "-", "cpu_ticks": 0, "ram_slots": 0, "ram_used": 0}
if PART == "C":
    sp = server_specs(conn)
sleep(2)
log("SPECS tier=" + str(sp.cpu_tier) + " ticks=" + str(sp.cpu_ticks) + " ram=" + str(sp.ram_slots) + "/" + str(sp.ram_used) + " ids=" + ids0)

mark("B-IN", t0)
poll("B", 10, t0)
mark("B-OUT", t0)

sleep(1)
if PART == "C":
    disconnect(conn)
sleep(2)

mark("C-IN", t0)
poll("C", 12, t0)
mark("C-OUT", t0)
log("T26 DONE part=" + PART + " - paste EVERY A / B / C line and EVERY MARK line")
